---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import CaseStudyCard from '../components/CaseStudyCard.astro';
import explorerShot from '../assets/intelligence-explorer.png';
import variantCoverage from '../assets/p2p-variant-coverage.png';
import gbsTornado from '../assets/gbs-tornado.png';
import daxAnswer from '../assets/dax-intelligence-answer.png';
import agenticMix from '../assets/gbs-agentic-mix.png';
import fpaForecastError from '../assets/fpa-forecast-error.png';
import fccaControlReview from '../assets/fcca-control-review.png';
import locationDashboard from '../assets/gbs-location-dashboard.png';
import tomDashboard from '../assets/gbs-tom-dashboard.png';

// This array is not the page order. Each card declares a `group`, and the
// `groups` array below decides which groups render and in what order; within a
// group the cards keep the order they have here. A card whose `group` matches
// no key renders nowhere, so the two lists have to stay in step.
const caseStudies = [
  {
    title: 'GBS Location Selection',
    summary:
      'Ranks the cities where finance shared-services work is genuinely advertised, on seven pillars of public data \u2014 and refuses to rank the ones the evidence cannot tell apart.',
    data: 'Public job postings, ILOSTAT earnings and employment, World Bank governance indicators, Eurostat regional accounts.',
    group: 'gbs',
    problem:
      'A location study arrives as three cities and a weighted scorecard. The weights were agreed in a workshop, and the weights are what chose the three cities \u2014 which is then presented to a steering committee as measurement.',
    build:
      "The first version rested on a sample searched for ‘finance operations’ and similar. Only 13% of those 2,159 postings carried any shared-services or capability-centre signal — the pillar meant to describe GBS was describing retained finance nine times out of ten. The population is now fetched for the delivery model instead, and every posting passes two independent classifiers. Eleven cities are scored on seven pillars of public data, then re-ranked 10,000 times across every defensible weighting, resampling each input inside its own measured error. Cities the draws cannot separate share a band rather than being given invented positions: seven of the fifty-five city pairs finish closer than 65/35. Ten further locations are priced on the five pillars that reach them and never ranked, because the two built from job postings stop at the feed’s country list.",
    stack: ['Python', 'NumPy', 'DuckDB', 'Matplotlib', 'JavaScript', 'pytest'],
    metricValue: '13%',
    metricLabel:
      'of the original sample was actually GBS or GCC work \u2014 the rest was retained finance, the work a shared-services centre exists not to do',
    implication:
      'Restricting the population changes which city leads: Warsaw dominated Poland on the broad sample because it is the largest finance job market, while Kraków leads on GBS and GCC work because it is the largest shared-services one. The ablation matters more than the ranking it produced — removing each pillar in turn shows that for a transactional hub the answer is a cost ranking wearing six other pillars, every one of which can be deleted without moving the shortlist, while five of seven move the judgment one.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/gbs-location-selection',
    demoUrl: 'https://morichtereur.github.io/location-dashboard/',
    figure: {
      src: locationDashboard,
      alt: 'The location dashboard in use. A headline reads \u201c5 cities finish level at the top\u201d; a toggle switches between a transactional hub and a judgment centre; seven sliders set the weight on cost, talent, governance, capability, overlap, durability and employer depth. The ranked exhibit shows Mumbai, Chennai, Pune, Bangalore and Hyderabad sharing band one, each with a flag, its posting count and its top-three frequency, and Pozna\u0144 opening band two.',
      caption:
        'The tool as it runs. Move a weight and the ranking, the bands and the sentence above them all move with it \u2014 and cities the draws cannot separate keep sharing a band instead of trading places.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'GBS Target Operating Model',
    summary:
      'Prices how often purchase-to-pay work crosses between delivery units, solves the assignment to proven optimality — and reports a price and a set of bands rather than a target operating model.',
    data: 'The same public SAP event log, plus the public wage panel and posting classifier from the two upstream studies.',
    group: 'gbs',
    problem:
      'A target operating model arrives as four columns on a slide — retained, captive, provider, automated — with the activities sorted into them by the people whose functions are being sorted. The expensive part is not where an activity sits. It is how often work crosses between columns, and nobody in the room has measured which activities actually follow each other.',
    build:
      'Measured the crossings from the same 1.6M-event SAP log: 498 directly-follows pairs across 11,973 variants, of which 681,352 transitions are priced once repeats and unavoidable vendor or system steps are excluded and reported. OR-Tools CP-SAT then assigns 29 activities to one delivery model and one city, proven optimal at every price, under four declared constraints — segregation of duties ported from the Finance Control Agent, minimum viable site size, contact coverage, and a cap on judgment work in a measured transactional hub. Both metrics were fixed in writing before the solve ran, and automation is allowed only where the log already shows an activity running in batch: Clear Invoice is 194,393 events with zero batch, so this model cannot automate it however much a slide would like to.',
    stack: ['Python', 'OR-Tools CP-SAT', 'DuckDB', 'LLM API', 'pytest'],
    metricValue: '13 of 29',
    metricLabel:
      'positions the cost model actually decides — the other 16 are ties, and the tool leaves them blank rather than filling them in',
    implication:
      'Concentrating purchase-to-pay pays only above USD 3.38 per handoff — the top half of the range declared before the sweep ran, not the middle of it. At the declared price of USD 1.50 the crossings already cost 1.85× the wage bill they coordinate, so the four-column slide is taking a position on a number larger than the one it optimises. Contact coverage — that somebody can answer a supplier inside their working day — is then worth more than the entire labour-arbitrage argument: remove it and 28 of 29 activities move and the bill falls by about half a million dollars. And retained is empty at every price, because nothing in the brief said authority over company spend has to sit inside the company; wanting one is not the same as having written it down.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/gbs-tom-assignment',
    demoUrl: 'https://morichtereur.github.io/tom-dashboard/',
    figure: {
      src: tomDashboard,
      alt: 'The target-operating-model tool in use. A headline reads “What a handoff costs decides the operating model”; an answer panel states that concentrating purchase-to-pay pays only above USD 3.38 per handoff, near the top edge of the range the study will defend, and that cost decides 13 of the 29 positions while the other 16 are ties left undecided. Below it a slider sets what one handoff costs, currently USD 1.50, with the defensible range and the 3.38 tipping point marked on the track.',
      caption:
        'The tool as it runs. Every stop on the slider is a price the model was actually solved at, so nothing on the page is an assignment the solver did not produce.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'P2P Process Mining',
    summary:
      'Mines a 1.6-million-event purchase-to-pay log to show the path each order actually took, and what the rework costs in days.',
    data: 'BPI Challenge 2019, a public SAP event log published for research at data.4tu.nl.',
    group: 'operations',
    problem:
      'Every finance transformation deck opens with the same sentence — the process is inefficient and highly manual. Almost nobody can say by how much, or where.',
    build:
      'Mined a 1.6M-event SAP purchase-to-pay log covering 251,734 purchase order line items, reconstructing the path each case actually took rather than the one the process documentation describes. Variant analysis, straight-through rate, the cycle-time cost of rework, and which sequence rules the system genuinely enforces versus merely documents.',
    stack: ['Python', 'DuckDB', 'LLM API', 'Matplotlib'],
    metricValue: '20%',
    metricLabel: 'of cases follow the most common path; rework costs 19.6 days',
    implication:
      'Cycle time here is an exception problem, not a capacity problem — and the expensive exceptions are not the frequent ones. A programme that targets the loudest rework activity would pick one of the cheapest. Prioritisation has to follow volume times penalty, which is only visible once the log is mined.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/p2p-process-mining',
    figure: {
      src: variantCoverage,
      alt: 'Cumulative share of cases covered as more process variants are included, on a log-scaled rank axis. The curve rises steeply then flattens: 50% of cases at rank 7, 90% at rank 232, 99% only at rank 9,456.',
      caption:
        'Coverage against variant rank. Half the cases sit in seven variants; the last percent needs nine thousand more.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'GBS Business Case',
    summary:
      'Turns that mined baseline into a shared-services business case, and sorts the remaining uncertainty into a work plan rather than a caveat.',
    data: 'The same public event log, plus published wage and cost statistics.',
    group: 'gbs',
    problem:
      'A shared-services business case usually rests on numbers guessed in a workshop — automation rate, rework share, handling time — presented as if they were measured.',
    build:
      'Built the case on the P2P Process Mining baseline instead: opportunity sizing and discounted cash flows from the measured straight-through rate and rework cost, then a 10,000-draw Monte Carlo across every assumption still uncertain. Each one is tagged whether diligence could resolve it or genuinely cannot yet, so the variance in the answer sorts into a work plan rather than a caveat.',
    stack: ['Python', 'DuckDB', 'NumPy', 'Matplotlib'],
    metricValue: '62%',
    metricLabel: 'of NPV variance comes from assumptions diligence can resolve — the work plan, not the caveat',
    implication:
      'At central assumptions the case does not clear the hurdle rate, which is a result rather than a dead end. The uncertainty that decides it is a handling-time study, not a vendor quote — so a week of desk observation moves the answer further than another round of estimating, and the model says which week to spend.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/gbs-business-case',
    figure: {
      src: gbsTornado,
      alt: 'Horizontal tornado chart of NPV sensitivity by assumption, low value to high value, bars coloured by whether diligence could measure the assumption or not. Minutes per rework touch, a measurable assumption, produces the widest swing — ahead of the implementation one-off cost.',
      caption:
        'The widest swing comes from a measurable assumption, not the implementation cost quote — the opposite of what a workshop case usually assumes.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'GBS Agentic Shift',
    summary:
      'Tests the claim that agentic AI is reshaping the GBS talent pyramid by reading 2,110 live job postings instead of asking practitioners.',
    data: 'Public job postings across ten markets.',
    group: 'gbs',
    problem:
      'McKinsey argues agentic AI is turning the GBS talent pyramid into a diamond — a shrinking transactional base and a new layer managing the agent force. It is a claim about what work looks like, cited far more often than it is checked.',
    build:
      "Read the claim off the labour market instead. Classified 2,110 live GBS and finance-operations postings across ten markets as transactional, judgment or agent-ops — a visible phrase taxonomy carries the headline, an LLM decides only the ambiguous residual, and the classifier's accuracy is measured against a hand-labelled gold set rather than asserted. Splitting by employer is the sharpest cut: captive GBS hires 38% transactional, third-party providers 84%. The base changed employer more than it shrank.",
    stack: ['Python', 'DuckDB', 'LLM API', 'Matplotlib'],
    metricValue: '2%',
    metricLabel: 'of postings ask for agent-ops skills — the diamond is not visible yet',
    implication:
      'Workforce plans built on the diamond thesis are ahead of the evidence. The live decision is not agent-ops recruitment but sourcing: a captive function that looks transformed may simply have moved its transactional base to a provider, and only the employer cut separates the two.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/gbs-agentic-shift',
    demoUrl: 'https://morichtereur.github.io/agentic-shift-dashboard/',
    figure: {
      src: agenticMix,
      alt: 'A single stacked bar across 2,110 GBS job postings: transactional 44%, judgment 55%, and a thin agent-ops sliver at 2% annotated in orange at the right edge.',
      caption:
        'Ten markets, one point-in-time cross-section. A measured 42.9% recall on the agent-ops class makes that 2% a lower bound rather than a ceiling — the classifier reports how wrong it is, so the finding can be read honestly.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'FP&A Decision Intelligence Accelerator',
    summary:
      'A driver-based forecast for adidas that ranks which drivers deserve management attention, backtested against two years of the company\u2019s own guidance.',
    data: 'adidas AG published annual reports and guidance.',
    group: 'operations',
    problem:
      'FP&A review cycles reliably produce analysis and much less reliably produce decisions. The pack lands, the variances are explained, the sensitivity table is admired — and the meeting ends without anyone naming which exposure is worth the next thirty minutes, who owns it, or what would have to change for the answer to move.',
    build:
      "A driver-based forecast for adidas, built from published filings, backtested against two vintages: FY2023\u2192FY2024 and FY2024\u2192FY2025, each using the initial guidance from the prior year's report rather than a figure revised part-way through the year it describes. On top of it, a decision layer. Each driver's financial exposure is computed by re-running the forecast across its plausible range, then ranked against two declared judgements — how firm the assumption is, and whether management can move it inside the year. Low controllability caps a driver at Monitor however large it is, because attention is a budget and should be spent where it converts. The engine is separated from the client: drivers, scenarios, thresholds and mappings live in configuration, a second client with materially different economics runs on the same code, and a third can be stood up from a spreadsheet without touching it.",
    stack: ['Python', 'NumPy', 'FastAPI', 'Next.js', 'LLM API', 'pytest', 'Vitest'],
    metricValue: '\u20ac900m',
    metricLabel:
      'unforecast working-capital release in FY2024 \u2014 the same assumption then cost \u20ac372m in FY2025, making it the largest error contributor in both years, in opposite directions',
    implication:
      "The ranking and the backtest agree, and neither was tuned to the other: the driver the materiality engine puts first for management attention is the one that has actually broken the forecast, twice. The second vintage also falsified a claim this project had been making. Carrying FY2023's 189% effective tax rate forward \u2014 tax on a near-zero pre-tax result in the write-off year \u2014 made the model look worse than a naive extrapolation on cash. That was the model being wrong, not the method being weak; the rule is guarded now, and the correction is in the repository history rather than quietly removed.",
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/fpa-decision-intelligence',
    demoUrl: 'https://fpa-decision-intelligence.vercel.app/priorities',
    figure: {
      src: fpaForecastError,
      alt: 'Two stacked bar charts on a shared scale, one per backtest vintage. FY2023 to FY2024: naive extrapolation errs 13.9% on revenue, 84.8% on operating profit and 72.2% on free cash flow, against 5.0%, 62.6% and 54.7% for the driver-based forecast. FY2024 to FY2025: naive errs 5.5%, 22.3% and 14.8%, against 3.1%, 14.9% and 3.4%. The driver-based bar is shorter in all six, and the FY2024 misses are several times the FY2025 ones.',
      caption:
        'Two vintages, not a track record. The driver-based forecast lands closer on all six metric-year pairs \u2014 a statement about a weak baseline, not about accuracy, since both methods undershot in both years and the FY2024 operating-profit forecast was wrong by 63%.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'Finance Control Agent',
    summary:
      'Runs two finance queues \u2014 month-end close and invoice-to-pay \u2014 on deterministic checks, with no language model allowed near arithmetic, matching or a tolerance decision.',
    data: 'A synthetic ledger generated by the project, plus its own written policy documents.',
    group: 'operations',
    problem:
      'Two finance processes have the same shape. Month-end close raises more exceptions than there are reviewer hours; accounts payable raises more invoice mismatches than there are clerk hours. Most items are benign, a few are not, and the failure mode is not a bad summary — it is money out of the door against a document nobody checked.',
    build:
      'Two modules on one auditable spine. Close: eighteen deterministic control checks over a synthetic ledger, policy sections retrieved with document and clause preserved, a validated decision, and a review gate that no model can talk its way past. Invoice-to-pay: twelve deterministic steps ending in a three-way match, with price and quantity normalised on both sides before anything is compared. One rule holds across both — no language model touches arithmetic, matching or a tolerance decision. The module that does the matching imports no provider and cannot call one, and a test asserts it.',
    stack: ['Python', 'LangChain', 'LlamaIndex', 'AWS Bedrock', 'DuckDB'],
    metricValue: '€46,812',
    metricLabel: 'overcharge that a naive three-way match clears as a 1.09% rounding difference',
    implication:
      'A vendor quietly drops a discount schedule and lifts the list price. Compare the printed prices and the gap is 1.09% — inside tolerance, so the invoice pays. Normalise both sides to a net price per base unit and it is 15.79%. That arithmetic is code on purpose; the model classifies and cites, it never computes. Across 334 invoices nothing was auto-cleared that should not have been, and across 60 close exceptions nothing requiring review was cleared — on two models, one costing a third of the other.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/finance-close-control-agent',
    demoUrl: 'https://morichtereur.github.io/finance-close-control-agent/',
    figure: {
      src: fccaControlReview,
      alt: 'A control review for one exception: a table of the four deterministic checks that fired with their severity, observed value and detail; the risk rating, finding and recommended action; a disposition of HUMAN REVIEW REQUIRED with the three reasons that forced it; and four cited policy sections with relevance scores and source file paths.',
      caption:
        'One close exception decided end to end on AWS Bedrock. The checks that fired carry their own thresholds, the disposition names the rule that forced it, and every policy section is quotable back to a file. Real output, typeset; two blocks abridged where marked.',
      blend: true,
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'GBS Intelligence Agent',
    summary:
      'Reads 69 public feeds, scores every article for strategic relevance, and puts only the top tier in a Monday brief.',
    data: 'Public RSS and news feeds from consulting firms, analyst houses and listed companies.',
    group: 'intelligence',
    problem:
      'Competitor and client intelligence reached the team through whoever happened to read the right article — usually weeks after it could have changed a proposal.',
    build:
      'A pipeline that reads 69 feeds across 33 consulting firms, analyst houses and client companies, then has the LLM score every article 1–3 for strategic relevance. Only a 3 reaches the Monday brief; everything below the cut stays searchable in a dashboard.',
    stack: ['Python', 'LLM API', 'SQLite'],
    metricValue: '16 weeks',
    metricLabel: 'delivered end to end, no manual step',
    implication:
      'Market awareness stops depending on who happened to read what. The summarising is the least of it — the point is that the filter runs on a Monday whether or not anyone remembers to look, which is the difference between a tool that was built and one that is used.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/gbs-intelligence-agent',
    demoUrl: 'https://morichtereur.github.io/gbs-intelligence-agent/',
    signal: { total: 1132, signal: 30 },
    figure: {
      src: explorerShot,
      alt: 'The Intelligence Explorer dashboard: a data-derived headline reading "Agentic AI leads competitor publishing", a firm-by-theme matrix with firm logos grouped into MBB, Big4 and Accenture clusters, theme filter tabs, and the start of a signal list split into market moves and research.',
      caption:
        'The Intelligence Explorer — an answer-first headline computed from the week’s data, a firm × theme matrix by cluster, and every signal linked to its source. The live demo runs on verified real publications; client intelligence stays in the private instance.',
    },
  },
  {
    title: 'DAX Intelligence',
    summary:
      'Answers one comparative question across 15 annual reports at once, and will not answer at all without naming the company and page it came from.',
    data: 'DAX 40 annual reports, as published.',
    group: 'intelligence',
    problem:
      'Answering one comparative question across a set of annual reports means opening every PDF and trusting your memory of what each one said.',
    build:
      'Hybrid retrieval across 15 DAX 40 annual reports: BM25 and dense search unioned rather than one re-scoring the other, then a cross-encoder re-ranks the combined pool. Every chunk carries the real page range its text came from rather than an estimate, so a citation resolves to a page that exists. The guardrail is the point — each citation is re-parsed out of the generated prose and checked against the excerpts the model was actually given before the answer is shown, and confidence bands calibrated against this corpus stop a query with no support in it from reaching the model at all. Precision@k, recall@k and a claim-level faithfulness judge are measured rather than asserted.',
    stack: ['Python', 'ChromaDB', 'BM25', 'sentence-transformers', 'LLM API', 'Streamlit', 'pytest'],
    metricValue: '15 reports',
    metricLabel: 'answerable in one query, with page citations',
    implication:
      'Analyst time shifts from finding a passage to judging it. The citation check is what makes that shift safe: an answer carrying a verified page number can go into a client document as it stands, while an unsourced one has to be found by hand anyway — which is the work it was supposed to save.',
    repoUrl: 'https://github.com/morichtereur/dax-intelligence',
    demoUrl: 'https://morichtereur.github.io/dax-dashboard/',
    figure: {
      src: daxAnswer,
      alt: 'A synthesised research memo comparing how Siemens and SAP frame AI investment strategy, each claim followed by an inline (Company Year, p.N) citation. Below it a green "VERIFIED — 8/8 citations matched retrieved excerpts exactly" badge, an export button, and the start of an audit trail table listing each retrieved excerpt with its page range and confidence score.',
      caption:
        'A real query against the 2025 filings. Every claim carries a page citation, and every citation is checked against the retrieved excerpt before it reaches the screen.',
    },
  },
];

const groups = [
  {
    key: 'gbs',
    heading: 'GBS & Shared Services',
    blurb:
      'Where to put a finance centre, whether the case for building one holds, and what work it will actually be doing.',
  },
  {
    key: 'operations',
    heading: 'Finance Operations & Planning',
    blurb:
      'Diagnosing a process from its own event log, running the controls that clear a month-end queue, and ranking what actually drives a forecast.',
  },
  {
    key: 'intelligence',
    heading: 'Market & Competitive Intelligence',
    blurb:
      'Reading more published material than a person has time for, and answering with the source attached.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout>
  <Hero />
  <Section id="case-studies" heading="Case Studies">
    <p class="provenance">
      <strong>Every project here runs on public data</strong> — official statistics,
      published annual reports, open job postings, public news feeds, and one public
      research dataset. Where a ledger was needed it is synthetic and generated by the
      project itself. <strong>No client data and no engagement material appears anywhere
      on this site</strong>, and each case study names its own sources so that is
      checkable rather than asserted.
    </p>

    <nav class="index" aria-label="Case study groups">
      {groups.map((g) => <a href={`#${g.key}`}>{g.heading}</a>)}
    </nav>
    {
      /* Grouped by what the work does rather than by subject. Some of these
         replace a number that was asserted in a workshop with one that was
         measured; the rest carry a decision or a queue that a person could not
         carry by hand. The subject labels would have sorted them into GBS and
         not-GBS, which says less. */
      groups.map((g) => (
        <div class="group" id={g.key}>
          <div class="group-head">
            <h3>{g.heading}</h3>
            <p>{g.blurb}</p>
          </div>
          {caseStudies.filter((cs) => cs.group === g.key).map((cs) => <CaseStudyCard {...cs} />)}
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </Section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  /* Stated before the first card, not in a footer. A reader who wonders whether
     this is client material should have the answer before the question forms,
     and each card repeats its own sources so the claim can be checked. */
  .provenance {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-6);
    padding-left: var(--space-4);
    border-left: 2px solid var(--color-accent);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }

  .provenance strong {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
  }

  /* Jump marks, not filters. This many cards do not need a control that hides
     most of them; they need somewhere to land. Sits in the mono register so
     it reads as navigation rather than as another heading. */
  .index {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--space-2) var(--space-5);
    margin-bottom: var(--space-7);
  }

  .index a {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--text-xs);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-label);
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
    padding-bottom: 0.15rem;
    transition: color 0.15s ease, border-color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .index a:hover,
  .index a:focus-visible {
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    border-bottom-color: currentColor;
  }

  /* The sticky nav would otherwise cover the heading the link lands on. */
  .group {
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-height) + var(--space-5));
  }

  .group + .group {
    margin-top: var(--space-8);
  }

  .group-head {
    max-width: 46rem;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-6);
    padding-top: var(--space-3);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--color-border-strong);
  }

  /* The group heading has to read as a level between the section label and a
     card title: quieter than the card, louder than body copy. Mono small-caps
     is the section register, so this takes the serif at reading size instead
     and earns its separation from the rule above rather than from weight. */
  .group-head h3 {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    /* Card titles are 24px. A group heading at 22px made the container quieter
       than its contents, which reads as an accident rather than a level. */
    font-size: 1.75rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: -0.015em;
    line-height: 1.25;
    color: var(--color-text-primary);
    text-wrap: balance;
  }

  .group-head p {
    margin: var(--space-2) 0 0;
    font-size: var(--text-sm);
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--color-text-secondary);
    text-wrap: pretty;
  }
</style>
